.th CR IV 7/12/77
.sh NAME
cr \*- card reader interface
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Cr
provides two devices:
.s3
.lp +10 4
1.	ASCII (well not quite) card reader (/dev/crN), and
.lp +10 4
2.	binary card reader (/dev/crbN).
.i0
.dt
.s3
.ul
ASCII Device
returns one character
for each column of the card and a newline character at the
end of the card (81 characters for an 80 column card).
The punch codes used conform to those used on the Cyber under
the UNSW Kronos operating system.
.s3
A number of special punchings are used in this system:
.s3
.ft I
.lp +10 7
code	meaning
.ft R
.s3
.lp +10 7
eor	end of record (punch 7-8-9) returns cntrl/r character (\022).
.s3
.lp +10 7
eof	end of file (punch 6-7-9)
returns cntrl/f character(\06).
.s3
.lp +10 7
eoi	end of information (punch 6-7-8-9) indicates a Unix end of file.
No character is returned but an end of file indication
is returned and the rest of the card is ignored.
Another read by a program after the end fo file has been
read will read the card following the end of information card
without closing and reopening the device (kludgy but useful
for spoolers).
.s3
.lp +10 7
cnvt	convert character set (punch 5-7-9) in column one.
If column two is blank then converts to O26 punch mode
and if column two is '9' then converts to O29 punch mode.
The convert card itself is not returned.
It
only effects those cards following up to the next convert
card or end of information.
The default mode is O29.
.i0
.dt
.s3
.ul
Card Codes for 029 Punch
.s3
.nf
	    punching in rows 1 - 7
	none 1   2   3   4   5   6   7
.s3
