.fi
.s3
.ul
Binary device
returns one word for each column (no newline).
An 80 column card returns 160 bytes of information.
The low order 12 bits of the word contain the binary 12 rows
of the card (bottom row 9 of card = least significant bit).
.s3
There are two versions of the card reader device handler for 
the two types of card reader available at UNSW:
.s3
.lp +10 4
1.	standard DEC unbuffered (column-by-column), and
.lp +10 4
2.	non-standard UNSW buffered (card-by-card).
.i0
.dt
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
In ASCII mode all columns are checked for validity -
at most one punching in rows 1 through 7
or a special control character and a valid
code according to the appropriate code table (O26 or O29)
above.
An invalid column will lead to a message
.s3
	cr\fIn\fR invalid - reread last card
.s3
This commonly occurs if a card is upside down.
.s3
On unbuffered card readers a column may
be missed leading to the following diagnostic
.s3
	cr\fIn\fR timing - reread last card
.sh BUGS
UNSW ASCII card codes are definitely unique.
.br
Binary mode should perhaps pack 4 columns into 3 words
as do some other systems.
This is not likely to be changed (ever).
.br
Certain ancient versions of the handler truncated
trailing blanks from the card in true Kronos fashion.
This was definitely a "no-no" for certain local uses
and has been rectified.
.br
A filter exists for removing
trailing blanks and adding tabs so that files
read from the card reader may be compressed.
